---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';
import workflow from '../../samples/github-workflow.yml.txt?raw';

const toc = [
   { id: 'needs', label: 'What a runner needs' },
   { id: 'workflow', label: 'A GitHub Actions job' },
   { id: 'fail', label: 'What fails the build' },
   { id: 'baseline', label: 'Start on a site with existing problems' },
   { id: 'sr', label: 'Check an announcement' },
   { id: 'tests', label: 'Run a test suite' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Run in CI"
   summary="<p>Run a11ied in CI so that a failed accessibility check fails the build. The runner needs a browser, and every screen reader command needs <code>--sr virtual</code>.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section>
      <h2 id="needs">What a runner needs</h2>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt>A browser</dt>
         <dd>Scans and the simulated screen reader run in Chrome, Edge, Brave, or Chromium. An image without one needs <code>npx playwright install chromium</code>.</dd>
         <dt><code>--sr virtual</code></dt>
         <dd>VoiceOver and NVDA do not run unattended. Pass <code>--sr virtual</code> to <code>sr start</code> and <code>sr walk</code>, or a macOS runner may try to start VoiceOver.</dd>
         <dt>A running app</dt>
         <dd>Start your server before the checks, or test files directly with a path.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         <code>a1 wcag</code>, <code>a1 pattern</code>, and <code>a1 search</code> read data
         that ships in the package. They need no browser and no network.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="workflow">A GitHub Actions job</h2>
      <pre data-label=".github/workflows/accessibility.yml"><code>{workflow.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <dl class="defs defs--wide">
         <dt><code>a1 doctor --strict</code></dt>
         <dd>Exits 3 when something is missing and prints the command that fixes it. Run it first, so a missing browser fails here with a clear message.</dd>
         <dt><code>a1 axe ... --fail-on serious</code></dt>
         <dd>Exits 4 on a serious or critical violation, which fails the job.</dd>
         <dt><code>a1 sr walk ... --out walk.md</code></dt>
         <dd>Saves what the screen reader said on the page. <code>if: always()</code> uploads it even when the scan failed.</dd>
      </dl>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="fail">What fails the build</h2>
      <p>
         Any step that exits with a code other than 0. A failed check exits 4. See
         <a href="/guides/scripting#exit-codes">exit codes</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
         A passing scan does not mean the page is accessible. It means no rule failed. Keep
         a screen reader check or a <a href="/guides/vitest">test</a> for the behavior a scan
         cannot see.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="baseline">Start on a site with existing problems</h2>
      <p>
         Commit a <a href="/guides/scripting#baseline">baseline</a> of the current
         violations. CI then fails only on a new one, and you fix the old ones over time.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 axe http://localhost:3000/checkout --level AA --baseline a11y-baseline.json</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="sr">Check an announcement</h2>
      <p>
         <code>sr goto</code> and <code>sr expect</code> exit 4 when nothing matches, so these
         lines fail the job when the button loses its name.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr virtual http://localhost:3000/checkout
a1 sr goto --role button --name "Pay now"
a1 sr expect "button, Pay now"
a1 sr stop</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="tests">Run a test suite</h2>
      <p>
         For more than a few checks, write them as tests with
         <a href="/guides/vitest"><code>a11ied/vitest</code></a> or
         <a href="/guides/testing"><code>a11ied/test</code></a>. They run in the same
         <code>npm test</code> step as the rest of your suite and need the same browser.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
